WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Every IPC endpoint needs ownership.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:10.040
Some component creates it, some identity owns it, some lifecycle governs it, and some cleanup rule removes or releases it.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:17.020
If those responsibilities are unclear, startup, shutdown, recovery, and permission review become much harder.

4
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.620
Named endpoints deserve special care.

5
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:28.280
Socket paths, pipe names, shared memory names, queue names, and service handles can become access points into the application.

6
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:34.940
Their names, locations, permissions, and cleanup rules determine who can discover or use them.

7
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.200
A name that is convenient for debugging may be too exposed for production.

8
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:48.760
Access can be shaped by filesystem permissions, directory placement, service accounts, inherited settings, platform defaults, and deployment scripts.

9
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.780
The code and deployment model should agree on who can connect and who cannot.

10
00:00:52.880 --> 00:01:00.000
If a service endpoint is meant only for a companion process, the storage location and permissions should reinforce that intent.

11
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:04.440
Descriptor and handle inheritance can expose channels unintentionally.

12
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:11.340
A child process may receive access to an IPC endpoint because the parent did not control what it inherited.

13
00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.520
That can make later behavior difficult to reason about, especially when helpers run with different roles or process lifetimes.

14
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:24.120
Stale endpoint files, abandoned shared memory regions, or orphaned queues can confuse recovery.

15
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:33.020
A service restart should know whether it is seeing a valid existing resource, a stale resource to clean up, or a sign that another instance is still running.

16
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.280
Endpoint exposure is not just a security topic; it is part of the application's reliability model.
